WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.922 --> 00:00:06.924
[waves crashing]

2
00:00:22.981 --> 00:00:25.275
[upbeat music playing]

3
00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:17.077
Sorry I'm late.

4
00:01:18.912 --> 00:01:20.080
Where are the kids?

5
00:01:20.706 --> 00:01:21.748
Uh. when I left.

6
00:01:21.832 --> 00:01:24.501
Hunter was making love with his iPad
on the terrace

7
00:01:24.585 --> 00:01:27.129
and Tori was ordering room service
like her life depended on it.

8
00:01:27.212 --> 00:01:28.755
I made dinner reservations.

9
00:01:28.839 --> 00:01:30.048
What are you gonna do?

10
00:01:30.841 --> 00:01:32.092
Best laid plans.

11
00:01:32.676 --> 00:01:36.096
- Hi. I will have a Negroni. please.
- Sure.

12
00:01:37.014 --> 00:01:39.474
- Oh. wow.
- It's my vacation drink.

13
00:01:39.558 --> 00:01:42.477
[chuckles] I thought too much rosé
was your vacation drink.

14
00:01:42.561 --> 00:01:45.939
Yeah. so I guess Negronis
are my "vacation is over" drink.

15
00:01:46.648 --> 00:01:47.733
Reentry's a bitch.

16
00:01:47.816 --> 00:01:49.276
I don't wanna go home.

17
00:01:49.818 --> 00:01:51.445
Hey. we're here till we're not.

18
00:01:51.528 --> 00:01:53.405
- What? Thank you.
- [bartender] Negroni for the lady.

19
00:01:53.488 --> 00:01:54.948
- Enjoy.
- That's my vacation philosophy.

20
00:01:55.032 --> 00:01:57.326
Wow. Coop. that's deep.

21
00:01:57.409 --> 00:01:59.203
You know that actually
doesn't mean anything. right?

22
00:01:59.286 --> 00:02:01.121
It will after your second Negroni.

23
00:02:01.205 --> 00:02:02.623
I may switch to mezcal.

24
00:02:02.706 --> 00:02:04.708
- Famous last words. [chuckles]
- [chuckles]

25
00:02:06.168 --> 00:02:07.586
There will be others.

26
00:02:07.669 --> 00:02:09.128
Last words?

27
00:02:09.213 --> 00:02:10.589
No. vacations like this.

28
00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:13.133
You think?

29
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.052
We're here till we're not.

30
00:02:15.135 --> 00:02:17.596
Oh. my God. Make it stop.

31
00:02:17.679 --> 00:02:20.182
Think about it. We've been very evolved…

32
00:02:20.974 --> 00:02:22.226
doing something like this.

33
00:02:22.309 --> 00:02:24.102
Easier when we're not sleeping
with each other's friends.

34
00:02:24.186 --> 00:02:26.396
- Hey. you did it first.
- You were charged with murder.

35
00:02:27.022 --> 00:02:28.315
Fair.

36
00:02:28.398 --> 00:02:29.983
- Can I have another one please?
- Wow.

37
00:02:30.067 --> 00:02:31.235
[bartender] Sí. señora.

38
00:02:32.277 --> 00:02:33.362
Put that down quick.

39
00:02:33.445 --> 00:02:35.239
Miles to go before I sleep.

40
00:02:36.406 --> 00:02:37.783
We're 50. Coop.

41
00:02:38.325 --> 00:02:40.160
- Damn.
- [chuckles]

42
00:02:40.244 --> 00:02:42.579
I really thought
I'd have it all figured out by now

43
00:02:42.663 --> 00:02:45.499
or at least I thought
I wouldn't be starting from scratch.

44
00:02:45.582 --> 00:02:49.294
Listen. this time last year.
I was looking at life in prison.

45
00:02:49.378 --> 00:02:52.548
And we could have lost everything.
so I think we're doing okay.

46
00:02:52.631 --> 00:02:54.925
What you're saying is.
you lowered the bar.

47
00:02:55.008 --> 00:02:57.553
- I obliterated the bar. There is no bar.
- [laughs]

48
00:02:59.304 --> 00:03:01.849
Let's get very. very. very drunk.

49
00:03:03.559 --> 00:03:04.852
Okay.

50
00:03:15.529 --> 00:03:16.989
[sucks teeth]

51
00:03:17.072 --> 00:03:18.907
[clicks tongue] Did I interrupt that?

52
00:03:18.991 --> 00:03:20.617
[chuckles] Not at all.

53
00:03:20.701 --> 00:03:22.578
Sitting here drinking Negronis.
You're looking to get laid.

54
00:03:22.661 --> 00:03:24.538
- Not my type.
- Bullshit.

55
00:03:25.372 --> 00:03:26.999
I would do her.

56
00:03:27.082 --> 00:03:28.417
Really?

57
00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.170
I mean. if that's the case.
I-I really wasn't prepared for that.

58
00:03:31.253 --> 00:03:33.130
I just. uh… I can rally.

59
00:03:33.213 --> 00:03:35.465
- You know. we're all consenting adults.
- In your dreams.

60
00:03:35.549 --> 00:03:37.509
- I've had that dream.
- Don't let me get in your way.

61
00:03:37.593 --> 00:03:39.261
You're not in my way.
You're not getting in my way.

62
00:03:39.344 --> 00:03:40.596
I mean. you're on vacation.

63
00:03:40.679 --> 00:03:41.889
If that's what you want to do.

64
00:03:41.972 --> 00:03:44.349
- damn it. you should do it.
- That's not what I want to do.

65
00:03:44.433 --> 00:03:46.852
I mean. the kids bailed.
It's your last night.

66
00:03:46.935 --> 00:03:48.937
We managed to make it
through the entire trip

67
00:03:49.021 --> 00:03:50.230
without falling into bed together.

68
00:03:50.314 --> 00:03:52.232
- Yay for us. by the way.
- Yay for us.

69
00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:54.443
This is America.

70
00:03:54.526 --> 00:03:57.237
- Mexico. actually.
- She's hot. you're well-preserved.

71
00:03:57.321 --> 00:03:58.488
[chuckles] Thank you.

72
00:03:58.572 --> 00:04:01.033
And if you wanna get yourself some.
you should get yourself some.

73
00:04:01.992 --> 00:04:04.161
If I wanted to do anything.
I would have done it already.

74
00:04:06.705 --> 00:04:08.123
That easy. is it?

75
00:04:10.709 --> 00:04:14.004
- Yeah. I'm gonna go.
- Where you going?

76
00:04:14.087 --> 00:04:15.464
What-What-What just happened?

77
00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:18.591
- Nothing happened. You're good.
- I'm good?

78
00:04:18.675 --> 00:04:21.803
- You say that. but--
- I'm not gonna sit here and…

79
00:04:23.639 --> 00:04:24.640
cockblock you.

80
00:04:24.723 --> 00:04:26.934
I feel like this conversation
just took a left turn.

81
00:04:27.017 --> 00:04:28.727
And you'd be right about that.

82
00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:34.525
Hey. I got us a table at Flora Farms.

83
00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:37.069
Do you have any idea how hard that was?

84
00:04:37.861 --> 00:04:39.029
Best laid plans. right?

85
00:04:44.368 --> 00:04:46.078
[Coop] There was a moment
when going on vacation

86
00:04:46.161 --> 00:04:48.038
with my ex-wife seemed inconceivable.

87
00:04:48.121 --> 00:04:49.540
[fireworks exploding]

88
00:04:49.623 --> 00:04:51.917
Turns out. there was good reason for that.

89
00:04:52.543 --> 00:04:55.546
But even having another argument
with Mel that I didn't fully understand

90
00:04:55.629 --> 00:04:58.715
couldn't stop me from relishing the fact
that I was drinking smooth whiskey

91
00:04:58.799 --> 00:05:03.846
on the beach at a five-star resort
when I could've been serving 25 to life.

92
00:05:03.929 --> 00:05:06.515
All things considered.
things were on the upswing.

93
00:05:08.392 --> 00:05:09.768
This is what happens…

94
00:05:10.686 --> 00:05:11.895
You get arrested for murder.

95
00:05:11.979 --> 00:05:13.105
You get exonerated.

96
00:05:13.188 --> 00:05:16.233
You get offered your old job back.
but you decide to go a different way.

97
00:05:16.316 --> 00:05:18.068
burning that bridge once and for all.

98
00:05:18.652 --> 00:05:22.906
Which means your interim income source
is now your primary income source.

99
00:05:23.699 --> 00:05:25.075
So. you level up

100
00:05:26.076 --> 00:05:27.202
and mend fences.

101
00:05:28.453 --> 00:05:31.331
Nothing buries the hatchet faster
than a solid revenue stream.

102
00:05:32.541 --> 00:05:35.627
But in a town where perception
is nine-tenths of the law.

103
00:05:36.378 --> 00:05:37.754
you wanna keep up appearances.

104
00:05:38.463 --> 00:05:41.675
Like having an office in Manhattan
to go to work in every day.

105
00:05:41.758 --> 00:05:44.469
Which helps get your co-parenting
back on track.

106
00:05:44.553 --> 00:05:45.554
Let's go.

107
00:05:46.597 --> 00:05:48.140
[tires screech]

108
00:05:49.933 --> 00:05:52.019
[Coop] And now that life
is feeling manageable.

109
00:05:52.102 --> 00:05:54.146
you can finally enjoy being single.

110
00:05:54.229 --> 00:05:55.522
♪ Hold tight ♪

111
00:05:56.106 --> 00:05:58.483
♪ We're in for nasty weather ♪

112
00:05:59.276 --> 00:06:03.405
♪ There has got to be a way ♪

113
00:06:04.156 --> 00:06:06.033
♪ Burning down the house ♪

114
00:06:06.116 --> 00:06:07.868
Which means nights on the town.

115
00:06:09.953 --> 00:06:11.747
And nights spent at home.

116
00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:13.373
["Burning Down the House" playing]

117
00:06:13.457 --> 00:06:15.459
♪ Watch out
You might get what you're after ♪

118
00:06:15.542 --> 00:06:19.379
♪ Cool babies
Strange but not a stranger ♪

119
00:06:19.463 --> 00:06:23.300
♪ I'm an ordinary guy ♪

120
00:06:23.383 --> 00:06:26.011
[moaning]

121
00:06:28.347 --> 00:06:32.059
[Coop] You couldn't say if it was
being arrested for murder or exonerated.

122
00:06:32.142 --> 00:06:36.522
but somehow the entire tawdry episode
has boosted your social cachet.

123
00:06:37.147 --> 00:06:39.107
Not so much for Sam Levitt.

124
00:06:39.191 --> 00:06:42.903
Her lawyer managed to plead her down
to a hefty fine and community service.

125
00:06:45.113 --> 00:06:46.615
She may have gotten off easy.

126
00:06:46.698 --> 00:06:48.700
but there's another law
in Westmont Village.

127
00:06:48.784 --> 00:06:49.868
[chattering. laughing]

128
00:06:49.952 --> 00:06:52.079
And that particular court shows no mercy.

129
00:06:53.413 --> 00:06:55.707
Life is better than it's been for a while.

130
00:06:55.791 --> 00:06:58.210
The family dynamic
is more or less working.

131
00:06:58.293 --> 00:06:59.753
Your kids are thriving.

132
00:06:59.837 --> 00:07:02.047
And if there are moments
where the simmering pain

133
00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:04.925
of your fractured family still threatens
to kick your ass…

134
00:07:05.968 --> 00:07:08.679
well. that's where those nights
I mentioned before come in.

135
00:07:08.762 --> 00:07:10.889
[moaning]

136
00:07:10.973 --> 00:07:14.810
[Coop] And failing that.
there's always scotch and a classic movie.

137
00:07:14.893 --> 00:07:17.604
[characters chattering on TV]

138
00:07:19.439 --> 00:07:21.900
[Coop]
You're finally feeling comfortable again.

139
00:07:21.984 --> 00:07:25.028
In control. Safe. Optimistic even.

140
00:07:30.909 --> 00:07:33.704
And as you already know
from painful experience.

141
00:07:34.872 --> 00:07:38.876
that's generally when life will serve up
a perfectly aimed kick in the crotch.

142
00:08:03.817 --> 00:08:04.818
[broker] Mr. Ashe.

143
00:08:05.402 --> 00:08:07.446
Hi. Reenie Hudson. We spoke on the phone.

144
00:08:07.529 --> 00:08:08.864
- Hi. Reenie. How are ya?
- Hi.

145
00:08:08.947 --> 00:08:10.574
Wow. nice car.

146
00:08:10.657 --> 00:08:11.992
Oh. you a car person?

147
00:08:12.075 --> 00:08:14.286
- I mean. not exactly. [chuckles]
- Oh. that's small talk.

148
00:08:14.369 --> 00:08:16.455
- Small talk. sorry. Thank you.
- Yeah. Okay. after you.

149
00:08:23.837 --> 00:08:24.838
Hello.

150
00:08:25.923 --> 00:08:27.883
Hi. Are you the listing broker?

151
00:08:27.966 --> 00:08:30.344
- Yes. Samantha Levitt. [chuckles]
- Hi. Owen Ashe.

152
00:08:30.427 --> 00:08:31.929
You don't look like a… a realtor.

153
00:08:32.011 --> 00:08:33.847
- Oh. [laughs]
- I mean that in the best possible way.

154
00:08:33.931 --> 00:08:35.390
Oh. I'm kinda new to it.

155
00:08:35.474 --> 00:08:38.268
- Oh. hey. Reenie.
- Hey. Samantha.

156
00:08:38.352 --> 00:08:40.062
[chuckles]

157
00:08:40.145 --> 00:08:41.647
So. Mr. Ashe.
should I show you around the house?

158
00:08:41.730 --> 00:08:43.565
- Oh. just Ashe.
- Okay. Ashe.

159
00:08:43.649 --> 00:08:46.443
Uh. yeah. I would. uh…
Yeah. I'd love that.

160
00:08:46.527 --> 00:08:48.278
But honestly.
I don't wanna waste your time

161
00:08:48.362 --> 00:08:52.032
'cause I just feel like there's no world
in which I'm buying this house.

162
00:08:52.115 --> 00:08:53.283
Oh. well. that was quick.

163
00:08:53.367 --> 00:08:56.411
No. it's just too small. Yeah. I mean.
it's… it's way too small actually.

164
00:08:56.495 --> 00:08:57.955
I mean. I'm standing here and…

165
00:08:58.038 --> 00:09:00.374
I don't know. I can already feel
the entire house. you know?

166
00:09:00.457 --> 00:09:02.543
I want something I can get lost in.

167
00:09:02.626 --> 00:09:05.879
And this. yeah. I don't know.
Some of these design elements…

168
00:09:05.963 --> 00:09:07.089
Yuck.

169
00:09:07.881 --> 00:09:08.966
Yeah. I don't know.

170
00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:12.094
I was pretty clear I was looking
for something much grander. you know?

171
00:09:12.177 --> 00:09:14.721
With a lot more oomph. some wow.

172
00:09:14.805 --> 00:09:17.391
- But this place isn't even remotely--
- It's my house.

173
00:09:17.474 --> 00:09:19.309
- It's your house?
- Mm-hmm.

174
00:09:19.393 --> 00:09:21.728
That would have been great information
to have before I arrived.

175
00:09:21.812 --> 00:09:25.649
[chuckles] I didn't know if she
was disclosing that fact. considering the…

176
00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:27.526
the circumstances.

177
00:09:27.609 --> 00:09:28.819
The circumstances?

178
00:09:29.862 --> 00:09:31.572
What Reenie is so subtly referring to

179
00:09:31.655 --> 00:09:34.199
is the fact that my husband
died here last year.

180
00:09:34.283 --> 00:09:36.994
Oh. wow. Well. I'm sorry for your loss.

181
00:09:37.077 --> 00:09:38.120
Thank you.

182
00:09:38.203 --> 00:09:40.956
Well. my wife died eight years ago.
so. you know. I'm in the club.

183
00:09:41.039 --> 00:09:43.333
- [Samantha] I'm sorry for your loss.
- Oh. thank you.

184
00:09:43.417 --> 00:09:44.835
How did he die?

185
00:09:45.419 --> 00:09:46.503
He killed himself.

186
00:09:47.171 --> 00:09:50.215
- What. here in the house?
- Pretty much right where you're standing.

187
00:09:52.092 --> 00:09:54.011
Wait a minute. This is that house?
That whole thing?

188
00:09:54.094 --> 00:09:55.804
- Mmm. Mm-hmm.
- Yeah. it made the news.

189
00:09:55.888 --> 00:09:57.431
They arrested you.

190
00:09:57.514 --> 00:09:58.515
Yes.

191
00:09:58.599 --> 00:10:01.268
- [Ashe] You're a dangerous woman.
- [Samantha. Ashe chuckling]

192
00:10:01.351 --> 00:10:03.312
Hey. I've had a couple brushes
with the law myself.

193
00:10:03.395 --> 00:10:05.230
Then I guess we're both in that club too.

194
00:10:05.981 --> 00:10:07.399
- I guess so.
- [chuckles]

195
00:10:07.482 --> 00:10:09.526
Okay. Well. um. Mr. Ashe.

196
00:10:09.610 --> 00:10:11.320
- we should probably get going.
- Yeah. yeah.

197
00:10:11.403 --> 00:10:12.946
Hey. uh. Samantha.

198
00:10:13.030 --> 00:10:15.199
Why would a broker who knows
I'm in a different league

199
00:10:15.282 --> 00:10:16.491
show me this house?

200
00:10:19.703 --> 00:10:21.038
It's her fluffer house.

201
00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:23.081
- A fluffer house?
- Mmm.

202
00:10:23.165 --> 00:10:26.084
[scoffs] Now. that's… that's not a thing.

203
00:10:26.168 --> 00:10:29.129
It makes the next houses she shows
you look even bigger

204
00:10:29.213 --> 00:10:30.672
and grander by comparison.

205
00:10:30.756 --> 00:10:32.799
- No. no. no.
- I see.

206
00:10:32.883 --> 00:10:35.594
I like to educate my clients
about the entire market

207
00:10:35.677 --> 00:10:37.262
before they consider a purchase.

208
00:10:37.346 --> 00:10:38.889
Is that something you do?

209
00:10:38.972 --> 00:10:40.933
Uh. hard to say.

210
00:10:41.016 --> 00:10:42.809
No one in this town
will give me a listing.

211
00:10:42.893 --> 00:10:44.561
[Ashe] What? Why not?

212
00:10:44.645 --> 00:10:47.898
Oh. right.
The whole convicted felon thing.

213
00:10:47.981 --> 00:10:50.275
It was pleaded down
to a few misdemeanors actually.

214
00:10:50.359 --> 00:10:52.402
- That's great. Good for you.
- Thank you. [chuckles]

215
00:10:52.486 --> 00:10:54.404
- Will you give me a minute?
- Sure.

216
00:10:54.488 --> 00:10:55.614
- Reenie.
- Yeah?

217
00:10:55.697 --> 00:10:57.658
Yeah. do you mind
if I chat with you for a sec?

218
00:11:01.954 --> 00:11:04.873
[Reenie. Ashe speak indistinctly]

219
00:11:18.846 --> 00:11:20.472
That's a 750S. right?

220
00:11:23.308 --> 00:11:25.561
- Yeah.
- It's a good car.

221
00:11:26.645 --> 00:11:28.730
- Yes. it is.
- [chuckles]

222
00:11:28.814 --> 00:11:29.940
Hey. um…

223
00:11:30.858 --> 00:11:32.734
I'm suddenly finding myself
in need of a realtor.

224
00:11:32.818 --> 00:11:34.069
How's your day looking?

225
00:11:41.493 --> 00:11:42.536
[sighs]

226
00:11:43.662 --> 00:11:44.663
- Yes.
- Hey. Reenie.

227
00:11:44.746 --> 00:11:46.415
You have the listing
for the Bisbee estate. right?

228
00:11:46.498 --> 00:11:49.960
- Yes. but this is not okay.
- Can you get us in in about ten minutes?

229
00:11:50.043 --> 00:11:51.253
[sighs]

230
00:11:51.336 --> 00:11:52.379
- Sure.
- Great.

231
00:11:52.462 --> 00:11:53.922
- I was planning to take--
- We're all set.

232
00:11:54.006 --> 00:11:55.632
Nice. Let's take my car.

233
00:11:56.133 --> 00:11:57.384
- Hey. Samantha--
- Sam.

234
00:11:57.968 --> 00:11:59.094
Okay. Sam.

235
00:11:59.720 --> 00:12:02.347
I was just wondering.
how many houses have you actually sold?

236
00:12:02.431 --> 00:12:05.309
Including this one. uh. none.

237
00:12:06.518 --> 00:12:07.603
Great.

238
00:12:19.573 --> 00:12:21.950
[theme song playing]

239
00:12:24.703 --> 00:12:30.083
♪ A paper shadow
In a house of stone ♪

240
00:12:30.167 --> 00:12:35.214
♪ The wooden flag
Of a drifting dreamboat ♪

241
00:12:35.297 --> 00:12:39.426
♪ I never knew
How far the ride was gonna go ♪

242
00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:42.971
♪ Till I looked back
At the smoke ♪

243
00:12:43.055 --> 00:12:48.227
♪ You can't keep up
With the Joneses ♪

244
00:12:48.310 --> 00:12:53.148
♪ Don't wanna run
For the roses ♪

245
00:12:53.232 --> 00:12:58.403
♪ I got my pocket of posies ♪

246
00:12:58.487 --> 00:13:00.989
♪ Sometimes I wonder why ♪

247
00:13:01.073 --> 00:13:03.534
♪ Sometimes I wonder why ♪

248
00:13:03.617 --> 00:13:08.747
♪ I just don't run
With the Joneses ♪

249
00:13:08.830 --> 00:13:13.877
♪ Don't wanna smell
The dying roses ♪

250
00:13:13.961 --> 00:13:18.966
♪ I got my pocket
Full of posies ♪

251
00:13:19.049 --> 00:13:24.012
♪ I don't keep up
With the Joneses ♪

252
00:13:56.086 --> 00:13:59.298
I'm Jeff Pratt. with the Princeton Alumni
Schools Committee.

253
00:13:59.381 --> 00:14:00.799
and I'll be conducting your interview.

254
00:14:00.883 --> 00:14:02.009
Mmm. Got it.

255
00:14:02.759 --> 00:14:04.970
I know your parents from back in the day.

256
00:14:05.053 --> 00:14:06.138
- Are they well?
- Yes.

257
00:14:06.221 --> 00:14:07.222
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

258
00:14:07.306 --> 00:14:10.058
So. Tori. Tell me. why Princeton?

259
00:14:10.851 --> 00:14:12.728
Aside from the fact
that both your parents went there.

260
00:14:13.812 --> 00:14:15.272
That's not enough?

261
00:14:15.355 --> 00:14:18.108
Well. um. put another way.

262
00:14:18.192 --> 00:14:22.029
what is it you're looking to get out
of your four years with us?

263
00:14:22.613 --> 00:14:25.616
Oh. are you… are you on the faculty there?

264
00:14:25.699 --> 00:14:28.785
- What? No.
- I just assumed when you said "us."

265
00:14:28.869 --> 00:14:31.872
- Sorry. [chuckles] Figure of speech.
- [chuckles] Oh. okay.

266
00:14:34.708 --> 00:14:36.960
There's no need to be nervous.

267
00:14:37.044 --> 00:14:39.046
It's just us talking here.

268
00:14:39.129 --> 00:14:41.840
Well. first of all. Jeff.

269
00:14:43.217 --> 00:14:47.262
I think we should acknowledge
the pro forma nature of this interview.

270
00:14:48.472 --> 00:14:52.851
I'm a legacy kid. on both sides.
whose father has been a consistent donor.

271
00:14:53.393 --> 00:14:55.187
And the reason he's been
a consistent donor.

272
00:14:55.270 --> 00:14:59.066
the reason so many graduates of the Ivies
continue to support their alma maters.

273
00:14:59.149 --> 00:15:01.443
is to make sure there's a spot
for their kids there.

274
00:15:02.361 --> 00:15:03.779
Because. well. let's be honest.

275
00:15:03.862 --> 00:15:05.656
there are much worthier charities
to support.

276
00:15:07.366 --> 00:15:09.701
You all torched the planet
in the name of capitalism.

277
00:15:09.785 --> 00:15:12.120
and now.
rather than try to undo the damage

278
00:15:12.204 --> 00:15:14.122
or feed the hungry or house the homeless.

279
00:15:14.206 --> 00:15:16.792
you're giving millions
to 300-year-old institutions

280
00:15:16.875 --> 00:15:20.671
riddled with systemic racism.
anti-Semitism. and sexism.

281
00:15:21.505 --> 00:15:23.465
in order to ensure
that yet another generation

282
00:15:23.549 --> 00:15:26.760
of privileged rich kids
will continue to hoard the lion's share

283
00:15:26.844 --> 00:15:29.680
of what's left
of the planet's remaining resources.

284
00:15:29.763 --> 00:15:32.266
while the unwashed masses
go to war over the scraps.

285
00:15:33.767 --> 00:15:35.352
So. I appreciate you taking the time.

286
00:15:36.687 --> 00:15:37.855
but let's be honest.

287
00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:40.566
We both know I'm going to be accepted

288
00:15:41.900 --> 00:15:44.403
because you all engineered the system
to do just that.

289
00:15:50.534 --> 00:15:52.327
I didn't engineer anything.

290
00:15:52.411 --> 00:15:53.579
Sorry.

291
00:15:54.621 --> 00:15:55.789
Figure of speech.

292
00:15:59.376 --> 00:16:01.211
Also. tennis. [chuckles]

293
00:16:02.129 --> 00:16:04.673
- What?
- I'm being recruited for tennis.

294
00:16:07.342 --> 00:16:08.844
I probably should have led with that.

295
00:16:25.569 --> 00:16:27.779
[Ashe] Now this is what I'm talkin' about!

296
00:16:39.583 --> 00:16:41.752
- How much?
- [Samantha] Twenty million.

297
00:16:41.835 --> 00:16:44.129
- But I'm sure there's some wiggle room.
- I'll take it.

298
00:16:45.130 --> 00:16:46.256
Okay.

299
00:16:46.340 --> 00:16:48.842
- [Reenie] So you're offering full asking?
- Yeah. in cash.

300
00:16:48.926 --> 00:16:50.511
But I wanna close by Monday.

301
00:16:50.594 --> 00:16:51.637
- [Reenie] Monday?
- Yeah.

302
00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:53.597
We're gonna need
at least a week for contracts.

303
00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:55.265
Then you're gonna wanna do
a thorough inspection--

304
00:16:55.349 --> 00:16:57.559
I'm gonna have a whole team
up here tomorrow to kick the tires.

305
00:16:57.643 --> 00:16:59.520
In the meantime.
you can get the paperwork to my lawyer.

306
00:16:59.603 --> 00:17:01.146
Hey. congratulations.

307
00:17:01.230 --> 00:17:03.065
- [chuckles]
- You just sold your first house.

308
00:17:03.148 --> 00:17:05.776
[chuckles] It… What is it you do again?

309
00:17:07.819 --> 00:17:10.989
[Elena] "We elect a US senator
for how many years?"

310
00:17:11.073 --> 00:17:12.532
- [radio static]
- Six.

311
00:17:12.616 --> 00:17:14.159
[Elena] Okay. [inhales sharply]

312
00:17:14.242 --> 00:17:17.496
Name five of the original 13 states.

313
00:17:17.579 --> 00:17:18.914
Colonies.

314
00:17:18.997 --> 00:17:20.332
New York. New Jersey.

315
00:17:20.415 --> 00:17:22.792
Connecticut. Massachusetts.

316
00:17:22.876 --> 00:17:24.627
Rhode Island. Delaware.

317
00:17:25.253 --> 00:17:26.505
New Hampshire.

318
00:17:27.339 --> 00:17:29.967
Maryland. Virginia.

319
00:17:30.843 --> 00:17:33.512
Pennsylvania. Georgia.

320
00:17:33.595 --> 00:17:35.472
That's 11.

321
00:17:35.556 --> 00:17:37.099
What am I forgetting?

322
00:17:37.182 --> 00:17:39.643
- How do you know all this?
- I watch a lot of Jeopardy!

323
00:17:44.648 --> 00:17:45.816
[breathes deeply]

324
00:17:47.651 --> 00:17:49.987
Ah. Guess what?

325
00:17:50.529 --> 00:17:51.572
[Elena] What?

326
00:17:52.739 --> 00:17:55.659
A drawer full of Cartier LOVE Bracelets.

327
00:17:56.326 --> 00:17:57.494
[Elena scoffs. grunts]

328
00:17:57.578 --> 00:18:00.831
I'm telling you. every woman in this town
wears like ten of them.

329
00:18:00.914 --> 00:18:03.458
I don't get it. They wouldn't be
caught dead wearing the same dress.

330
00:18:03.542 --> 00:18:05.335
but they all need to have this one thing.

331
00:18:05.419 --> 00:18:07.254
It's like a requirement or something.

332
00:18:07.337 --> 00:18:10.007
You want me to grab you a couple?
They'll never miss 'em.

333
00:18:10.090 --> 00:18:11.550
That's not a club I need to join.

334
00:18:11.633 --> 00:18:13.719
Okay? Just find the pen.

335
00:18:13.802 --> 00:18:16.305
It's in the office
behind the primary bedroom.

336
00:18:17.139 --> 00:18:18.891
North and South Carolina.

337
00:18:18.974 --> 00:18:20.976
- What?
- The other two colonies.

338
00:18:21.059 --> 00:18:24.062
[chuckles] Okay.
Now you're just showing off.

339
00:18:32.029 --> 00:18:33.488
[Coop] It's not in the desk.

340
00:18:37.826 --> 00:18:39.912
Oh. Bingo.

341
00:18:39.995 --> 00:18:41.163
Drawer full of pens.

342
00:18:43.123 --> 00:18:47.669
Y-You sure he would keep something
this valuable in a mess like this?

343
00:18:47.753 --> 00:18:50.464
Natalia says he gets a crazy expensive pen

344
00:18:50.547 --> 00:18:53.008
every time he signs a deal
and then just throws it in a drawer.

345
00:18:53.091 --> 00:18:54.843
It's like some sort of ritual.

346
00:18:54.927 --> 00:18:55.928
All right.

347
00:18:57.721 --> 00:18:58.722
Found it.

348
00:18:59.306 --> 00:19:02.184
The Etoile de Montblanc
Joailliere Fountain Pen.

349
00:19:02.893 --> 00:19:05.020
With just 15 produced worldwide.

350
00:19:05.103 --> 00:19:09.107
this limited edition is crafted
entirely out of 18-karat rose gold.

351
00:19:10.192 --> 00:19:12.569
Adorned with Cognac diamonds

352
00:19:12.653 --> 00:19:16.281
and a nib that supposedly glides
more smoothly than RIMOWA wheels.

353
00:19:16.365 --> 00:19:17.991
it's not just a pen

354
00:19:19.201 --> 00:19:24.081
but an aspirational work of art
reselling for $165.000.

355
00:19:24.164 --> 00:19:25.499
I'm not sure what's worse.

356
00:19:25.582 --> 00:19:27.292
That someone pays that for a pen

357
00:19:27.376 --> 00:19:30.170
or that he tosses it into
a cluttered drawer with 30 others.

358
00:19:30.254 --> 00:19:32.923
Either way.
a guy like that deserves to lose it.

359
00:19:33.006 --> 00:19:34.716
- Shit.
- What?

360
00:19:34.800 --> 00:19:36.844
I dropped the fucking thing.
Hold on. Let me get it.

361
00:19:36.927 --> 00:19:38.554
[groans] I got it.

362
00:19:39.471 --> 00:19:41.098
[grunts. groans]

363
00:19:41.723 --> 00:19:43.517
Coop? What happened?

364
00:19:43.600 --> 00:19:44.601
Coop?

365
00:19:44.685 --> 00:19:46.854
- [Coop groaning]
- What is it?

366
00:19:46.937 --> 00:19:49.606
[groans] My back went out. [groaning]

367
00:19:49.690 --> 00:19:51.441
It really fucking hurts.

368
00:19:51.525 --> 00:19:54.152
- [winces] I can't move.
- What do you mean you can't move?

369
00:19:54.236 --> 00:19:56.321
- Just get out here.
- I mean I can't move.

370
00:19:56.405 --> 00:19:59.241
I… [panting] …I'm lying on my back.

371
00:19:59.324 --> 00:20:00.617
You can't be serious. Coop.

372
00:20:00.701 --> 00:20:02.786
Do I sound like I'm fucking joking?

373
00:20:02.870 --> 00:20:05.038
Shit. Okay. I'm coming in.

374
00:20:05.122 --> 00:20:06.999
No! [groans]

375
00:20:07.666 --> 00:20:08.959
[narrating] My back had been seizing up

376
00:20:09.042 --> 00:20:11.587
ever since Christian's art thugs
worked me over last year.

377
00:20:11.670 --> 00:20:14.756
Usually a cocktail of painkillers
and muscle relaxants would do the trick.

378
00:20:14.840 --> 00:20:16.508
and a few days later. I'd be golden.

379
00:20:16.592 --> 00:20:18.260
But I didn't have a few days.

380
00:20:18.343 --> 00:20:21.096
I had less than two hours before
the people who lived there would be back.

381
00:20:21.180 --> 00:20:22.806
And when it came to
thinking up an explanation

382
00:20:22.890 --> 00:20:24.558
for how I came to be lying on their floor.

383
00:20:24.641 --> 00:20:25.851
I was drawing a blank.

384
00:20:25.934 --> 00:20:27.686
Okay. Shit. We gotta move.

385
00:20:27.769 --> 00:20:29.980
Okay. Okay.
Just-Just help-help me up here.

386
00:20:30.063 --> 00:20:31.481
- Okay.
- Just. uh…

387
00:20:31.565 --> 00:20:32.983
[grunts. groans] Fuck. no.

388
00:20:33.066 --> 00:20:35.110
- Sorry. Okay. okay. okay.
- Fuck. fuck. Oh. no.

389
00:20:35.194 --> 00:20:36.820
[Coop breathes heavily]

390
00:20:36.904 --> 00:20:38.238
[groaning]

391
00:20:38.322 --> 00:20:40.991
[groans] God.
you're like dead fucking weight.

392
00:20:41.074 --> 00:20:44.578
- I can't move you. Can you crawl?
- [sighs] No. I can't even roll over.

393
00:20:44.661 --> 00:20:46.747
- [sighs] This is crazy.
- Just give me a few minutes.

394
00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:48.457
- We don't have a few minutes.
- Well. then go.

395
00:20:48.540 --> 00:20:50.292
- I'll figure something out.
- I'm not gonna bail on you.

396
00:20:50.375 --> 00:20:51.710
Well. I can't move.

397
00:20:51.793 --> 00:20:54.129
- We're gonna have to call someone.
- No. No.

398
00:20:54.213 --> 00:20:56.089
- You can't move!
- [groans]

399
00:20:56.173 --> 00:20:59.468
- [grunts] Fuck! [groaning] Fuck.
- Come on. Coop.

400
00:20:59.551 --> 00:21:01.595
[panting]

401
00:21:02.930 --> 00:21:05.474
- We have to call someone.
- No shit.

402
00:21:06.475 --> 00:21:08.560
- [panting]
- Who?

403
00:21:10.062 --> 00:21:11.772
Okay. What the fuck?

404
00:21:11.855 --> 00:21:14.233
I'll explain later. Can you…
Can you just please help me up?

405
00:21:14.316 --> 00:21:17.110
You'll explain what you're doing
on the floor of some random house

406
00:21:17.194 --> 00:21:18.403
with Nick's housekeeper?

407
00:21:18.487 --> 00:21:19.488
Elena.

408
00:21:19.571 --> 00:21:20.572
I know your name.

409
00:21:21.198 --> 00:21:23.825
Question… Whose house is this?

410
00:21:23.909 --> 00:21:25.786
- The Hemmings'.
- [Barney] And you know them how?

411
00:21:25.869 --> 00:21:26.995
We don't.

412
00:21:28.330 --> 00:21:29.998
"We don't." "We don't."

413
00:21:30.082 --> 00:21:32.584
- "We"? Really? "We"?
- Can we hurry this up. please?

414
00:21:32.668 --> 00:21:34.545
- We need to move.
- [Barney] Just so I'm clear.

415
00:21:34.628 --> 00:21:37.214
and I'm sorry
if I've misread the situation here.

416
00:21:37.297 --> 00:21:39.216
we are currently trespassing?

417
00:21:39.299 --> 00:21:41.385
- Correct.
- And why would we be doing that?

418
00:21:41.468 --> 00:21:43.303
Okay. They're gonna be home any minute.

419
00:21:43.387 --> 00:21:45.264
So can we finish up this Q and A later?

420
00:21:45.347 --> 00:21:46.849
Q and A? [scoffs]

421
00:21:46.932 --> 00:21:48.684
[chuckles] That's… That's funny.

422
00:21:48.767 --> 00:21:50.060
She's funny. Who knew?

423
00:21:50.143 --> 00:21:51.728
Barney! Please?

424
00:21:51.812 --> 00:21:53.105
[sighs]

425
00:21:54.439 --> 00:21:55.440
Okay.

426
00:21:55.524 --> 00:21:58.277
[sighs] You grab his left.
I'll grab his right. On three.

427
00:21:58.986 --> 00:22:00.946
- One. two. three. [grunts]
- Two.

428
00:22:01.029 --> 00:22:03.782
[groaning. grunts]

429
00:22:03.866 --> 00:22:05.576
[Elena sighs]
When does your sister get home?

430
00:22:05.659 --> 00:22:08.579
She's gonna be playing
for another couple hours at least.

431
00:22:08.662 --> 00:22:10.205
[Elena] Okay. Do you have pills for this?

432
00:22:10.289 --> 00:22:11.999
[Coop] Yeah.
Upstairs in the medicine cabinet.

433
00:22:12.082 --> 00:22:14.376
There's Flexeril and Percocet.
Please. Both of them.

434
00:22:14.459 --> 00:22:15.460
[Elena] Yep.

435
00:22:16.962 --> 00:22:18.714
[exhales deeply]

436
00:22:19.590 --> 00:22:23.677
So instead of taking back your job
at Bailey Russell.

437
00:22:24.261 --> 00:22:27.431
a job that would pay you seven
or eight figures annually before bonus.

438
00:22:27.514 --> 00:22:30.767
you're. um. robbing houses
with Nick's housekeeper?

439
00:22:30.851 --> 00:22:33.103
- Elena.
- I know her name.

440
00:22:36.690 --> 00:22:39.860
- I take it you two are. um… mm-mmm…
- What? No. What?

441
00:22:39.943 --> 00:22:41.612
Oh. yeah. yeah. yeah. Because that…

442
00:22:41.695 --> 00:22:44.698
that would be the crazy part
in this whole fucked up scenario.

443
00:22:44.781 --> 00:22:46.158
I know it's a lot to absorb.

444
00:22:46.241 --> 00:22:47.993
[Barney] I'm not absorbing anything.

445
00:22:48.076 --> 00:22:49.077
You know why?

446
00:22:49.161 --> 00:22:50.913
Because it doesn't make any fucking sense.

447
00:22:50.996 --> 00:22:54.541
I mean. if this were some kind
of sex kink thing. then fine.

448
00:22:54.625 --> 00:22:57.586
I mean. not fine. but at least I'd be able
to wrap my brain around it.

449
00:22:57.669 --> 00:23:00.047
But if not that. then-then what?
What is this?

450
00:23:00.130 --> 00:23:03.759
Is this some sort of elaborate.
three-stage midlife crisis?

451
00:23:03.842 --> 00:23:05.093
You know. divorce wasn't enough.

452
00:23:05.177 --> 00:23:06.512
Losing your job wasn't enough.

453
00:23:06.595 --> 00:23:08.305
Getting arrested for murder wasn't…

454
00:23:09.723 --> 00:23:10.891
Oh. my God.

455
00:23:13.769 --> 00:23:15.062
Coop. did you kill Paul Levitt?

456
00:23:15.145 --> 00:23:16.855
No. What are you talking about?

457
00:23:16.939 --> 00:23:18.982
I don't know what I'm talking about!

458
00:23:19.066 --> 00:23:23.111
My best friend just told me
he's some sort of suburban cat burglar.

459
00:23:23.195 --> 00:23:25.822
I mean. how do you even move this stuff?
Do you have a fence?

460
00:23:25.906 --> 00:23:27.115
No. You know… No.

461
00:23:27.908 --> 00:23:29.576
Don't tell me. I don't wanna know.

462
00:23:34.581 --> 00:23:35.707
Wait a minute.

463
00:23:39.294 --> 00:23:40.546
Coop.

464
00:23:42.589 --> 00:23:44.800
Those guys who beat you up…

465
00:23:46.385 --> 00:23:47.845
who ran me over…

466
00:23:49.012 --> 00:23:50.889
that was connected to this?

467
00:23:53.100 --> 00:23:54.726
I could've been fucking killed.

468
00:23:55.519 --> 00:23:56.645
I thought we had moved past this.

469
00:23:56.728 --> 00:23:58.772
I thought you weren't
a motherfucking thief.

470
00:24:00.148 --> 00:24:02.234
[sighs] What a relief.

471
00:24:03.527 --> 00:24:04.736
[Coop sighs]

472
00:24:06.655 --> 00:24:08.073
- [mutters] Hmm.
- Thank you.

473
00:24:12.953 --> 00:24:14.997
- What?
- Oh. nothing. I just…

474
00:24:15.581 --> 00:24:17.749
I just can't wait to hear
this meet-cute story.

475
00:24:17.833 --> 00:24:19.168
Do you have a problem with me?

476
00:24:19.251 --> 00:24:21.128
Of course I have a problem with you.

477
00:24:21.211 --> 00:24:23.714
- I have a problem with all of this!
- Don't yell at me!

478
00:24:23.797 --> 00:24:26.383
I'm not yelling at you!
I'm yelling near you!

479
00:24:26.466 --> 00:24:28.719
You're filling the whole room
with your fucking voice!

480
00:24:28.802 --> 00:24:31.763
Guys! Enough! Goddamn it.

481
00:24:33.140 --> 00:24:35.058
I got to get the fuck out of here.

482
00:24:35.142 --> 00:24:36.310
Barney.

483
00:24:36.393 --> 00:24:38.020
- Barney.
- What?

484
00:24:39.771 --> 00:24:41.148
Thank you for coming to get me.

485
00:24:41.773 --> 00:24:42.941
Fuck you. Coop.

486
00:24:43.609 --> 00:24:44.902
Fuck you very much.

487
00:24:48.697 --> 00:24:50.449
- Fuck!
- [door slams]

488
00:24:50.532 --> 00:24:53.243
That actually went a lot better
than I thought it was going to.

489
00:24:53.327 --> 00:24:56.622
- I know. You can't… [laughs]
- [laughs] And they were like… Yeah.

490
00:24:56.705 --> 00:24:57.748
Thanks.

491
00:24:58.290 --> 00:25:00.167
- Just give us a second. Thanks.
- Mm-hmm.

492
00:25:02.544 --> 00:25:03.545
[laughs]

493
00:25:03.629 --> 00:25:05.255
- I feel like they're kicking us out.
- I know.

494
00:25:05.339 --> 00:25:06.924
We do seem to be closing the place down.

495
00:25:07.007 --> 00:25:09.092
- [laughs]
- [sighs]

496
00:25:09.968 --> 00:25:12.846
[inhales deeply. sighs]

497
00:25:12.930 --> 00:25:13.931
What?

498
00:25:14.640 --> 00:25:16.016
I really can't remember the last time

499
00:25:16.099 --> 00:25:18.602
I enjoyed myself just talking
to someone like this.

500
00:25:18.685 --> 00:25:21.688
I have been exceptionally witty tonight.

501
00:25:21.772 --> 00:25:24.608
- Oh. really? Is that what it is?
- Oh. yeah. I brought my best material.

502
00:25:24.691 --> 00:25:27.819
- So it's all downhill from here?
- Oh. yeah. pretty much.

503
00:25:27.903 --> 00:25:29.821
[both laughing]

504
00:25:29.905 --> 00:25:31.156
God.

505
00:25:32.574 --> 00:25:35.661
When… When you get divorced.

506
00:25:36.411 --> 00:25:39.581
I feel like you're so focused
on the act of dissolving the marriage.

507
00:25:39.665 --> 00:25:42.209
you don't think about the complications
that are gonna come afterwards.

508
00:25:42.292 --> 00:25:46.547
So when they finally do come.
they kind of knock you on your ass.

509
00:25:46.630 --> 00:25:49.466
So it's been complicated?

510
00:25:49.550 --> 00:25:51.677
You could say that. yeah.

511
00:25:51.760 --> 00:25:53.303
But you're over it?

512
00:25:53.387 --> 00:25:54.596
I'm working on it.

513
00:25:55.639 --> 00:25:58.225
What about him? Are you over him?

514
00:25:58.308 --> 00:25:59.393
Who?

515
00:25:59.476 --> 00:26:01.645
[both laughing]

516
00:26:04.690 --> 00:26:05.732
I am.

517
00:26:06.942 --> 00:26:09.194
Absolutely. And then some.

518
00:26:09.987 --> 00:26:11.363
So you're conflicted?

519
00:26:11.446 --> 00:26:14.032
[both laughing]

520
00:26:15.367 --> 00:26:17.828
Well. I mean.
the way I see it… [clears throat]

521
00:26:18.662 --> 00:26:20.330
…is if we had known in advance

522
00:26:20.414 --> 00:26:23.041
how complicated our divorces
were gonna be.

523
00:26:23.125 --> 00:26:27.754
emotionally. financially.
fucking logistically…

524
00:26:28.422 --> 00:26:33.260
we'd all probably just give up
and still be in our lousy marriages.

525
00:26:33.343 --> 00:26:36.013
[chuckles] Then where would we be?

526
00:26:38.223 --> 00:26:40.225
- Not here.
- Exactly.

527
00:26:40.809 --> 00:26:43.854
Well. given we are here.
I'm just wondering…

528
00:26:46.607 --> 00:26:48.108
what happens next?

529
00:26:48.192 --> 00:26:50.068
[breathing heavily]

530
00:27:00.370 --> 00:27:03.165
[moaning. panting]

531
00:27:12.549 --> 00:27:14.718
- Wait. wait. wait.
- What? What? What?

532
00:27:14.801 --> 00:27:18.680
- [chuckles] I'm sorry.
- [panting] No. It's okay. It's all right.

533
00:27:20.182 --> 00:27:21.850
You all right? [panting]

534
00:27:21.934 --> 00:27:24.728
Yes. I'm sorry. I just…
I suddenly don't feel well.

535
00:27:24.811 --> 00:27:27.773
- Oh. God. It's okay. Uh… Is this. uh…
- I mean…

536
00:27:27.856 --> 00:27:29.441
[inhales sharply] Something I did?

537
00:27:29.525 --> 00:27:32.694
I promise I'm not blowing you off.

538
00:27:32.778 --> 00:27:35.239
- Okay.
- I was very much with the program.

539
00:27:35.322 --> 00:27:36.698
- Okay.
- Just…

540
00:27:36.782 --> 00:27:38.450
Listen. hey. it's fine. It's fine.

541
00:27:39.076 --> 00:27:40.494
Why don't I take you home?

542
00:27:50.254 --> 00:27:51.922
[Mel] I'm home!

543
00:27:53.382 --> 00:27:57.636
So if anybody's in a state of undress
or making out.

544
00:27:58.220 --> 00:28:01.682
please take the appropriate measures now.

545
00:28:04.810 --> 00:28:05.978
Hi. baby.

546
00:28:07.729 --> 00:28:08.856
Hey. Mom.

547
00:28:08.939 --> 00:28:11.400
Where's Morgan?
I thought you were hanging out tonight?

548
00:28:12.025 --> 00:28:13.151
No. it's just me.

549
00:28:13.235 --> 00:28:15.112
- Hi.
- They got into a fight.

550
00:28:15.195 --> 00:28:16.405
No. it wasn't a fight.

551
00:28:16.488 --> 00:28:17.739
It sounded like a fight.

552
00:28:18.740 --> 00:28:20.409
[Mel] Well. that stinks.

553
00:28:23.203 --> 00:28:24.204
[grunts]

554
00:28:27.708 --> 00:28:29.751
Who can fight with this face?

555
00:28:29.835 --> 00:28:31.837
I think you might be slightly biased.

556
00:28:31.920 --> 00:28:34.965
Well. according to Morgan. I'm an asshole.

557
00:28:35.841 --> 00:28:36.842
I will cut her.

558
00:28:38.135 --> 00:28:39.219
How was your date?

559
00:28:39.303 --> 00:28:40.971
I was thinking about hanging it up.

560
00:28:41.889 --> 00:28:44.183
- Yeah? Me too.
- Perfect.

561
00:28:45.017 --> 00:28:46.435
You can live with me forever.

562
00:28:47.686 --> 00:28:50.731
Take care of me when I am old and infirm.

563
00:28:50.814 --> 00:28:52.900
Okay. But I'm not
gonna change your diapers.

564
00:28:53.650 --> 00:28:54.651
Fair.

565
00:28:54.735 --> 00:28:57.029
We can have a ton of cats.

566
00:28:57.112 --> 00:28:59.239
- It'll be great.
- [Hunter groans]

567
00:29:03.619 --> 00:29:05.913
[serene music playing]

568
00:29:16.131 --> 00:29:17.382
[groans]

569
00:29:19.051 --> 00:29:20.719
Nothing made you more aware of being alone

570
00:29:20.802 --> 00:29:23.514
than when you needed help
getting your ass out of bed.

571
00:29:25.682 --> 00:29:30.395
My back was already feeling better.
which is to say. it hurt like hell.

572
00:29:30.938 --> 00:29:32.898
[sighs. groans]

573
00:29:34.274 --> 00:29:37.778
And as much as I wanted to blame it all
on that beating I took last year.

574
00:29:37.861 --> 00:29:40.572
the ugly truth was that I had a bad back.

575
00:29:40.656 --> 00:29:44.034
and a shrinking bladder.
and being middle-aged sucked.

576
00:29:44.117 --> 00:29:46.328
[urinating]

577
00:29:46.411 --> 00:29:48.830
[classical music playing]

578
00:30:26.159 --> 00:30:28.161
The new place is beautiful.

579
00:30:28.245 --> 00:30:29.496
Congratulations.

580
00:30:30.831 --> 00:30:31.832
Thank you.

581
00:30:36.753 --> 00:30:39.756
"A deluxe apartment in the sky."
[chuckles]

582
00:30:42.676 --> 00:30:43.760
I'm sorry?

583
00:30:44.303 --> 00:30:47.389
Uh. it's The Jeffersons…

584
00:30:48.724 --> 00:30:49.725
You-You remember?

585
00:30:49.808 --> 00:30:51.518
It was a television show
from when I was a…

586
00:30:51.602 --> 00:30:53.228
You know what? Never mind. It's…

587
00:30:56.398 --> 00:30:57.774
Grace is worried about you.

588
00:30:57.858 --> 00:30:59.776
Well. there's nothing to worry about. so…

589
00:30:59.860 --> 00:31:01.612
We wouldn't be here if that were true.

590
00:31:02.821 --> 00:31:04.656
Well. anyone can have a bad year.

591
00:31:04.740 --> 00:31:06.742
Your business model is flawed.

592
00:31:06.825 --> 00:31:12.289
Your risk is not properly managed.
You depend too much on too few clients.

593
00:31:12.372 --> 00:31:15.834
One of them has a bad year
and it hits your bottom line too hard.

594
00:31:15.918 --> 00:31:18.003
Respectfully. um.

595
00:31:18.086 --> 00:31:22.424
the art of wealth management
is choosing clients like stocks.

596
00:31:22.508 --> 00:31:26.303
You know. you make educated decisions
based on a wide array of data points.

597
00:31:26.386 --> 00:31:29.056
For instance. Nick Brandes.
one of my largest clients.

598
00:31:29.139 --> 00:31:30.849
he just signed a major broadcasting deal.

599
00:31:31.433 --> 00:31:33.143
And what happens if he leaves you?

600
00:31:33.227 --> 00:31:34.311
Or gets hit by a truck?

601
00:31:34.394 --> 00:31:35.521
Um. Mr. Park-- [stammers]

602
00:31:35.604 --> 00:31:37.064
You rely too much on other people

603
00:31:37.147 --> 00:31:39.358
making their money instead of
you making your money yourself.

604
00:31:39.441 --> 00:31:41.276
I-I make my money…

605
00:31:42.402 --> 00:31:45.906
by providing
a valuable and exclusive service.

606
00:31:47.574 --> 00:31:51.453
I have relationships
across every financial sector.

607
00:31:51.537 --> 00:31:54.164
You don't work
hard enough for what you have.

608
00:31:54.248 --> 00:31:56.708
Says the man who inherited
his business from his father.

609
00:31:56.792 --> 00:31:58.168
[chuckles]

610
00:32:00.921 --> 00:32:02.965
And I have no sons to do the same.

611
00:32:03.882 --> 00:32:05.551
- I thought maybe Grace--
- She doesn't want it.

612
00:32:06.134 --> 00:32:07.219
She wants it…

613
00:32:08.804 --> 00:32:09.972
for you.

614
00:32:10.556 --> 00:32:14.268
[chuckles] So… So you're. uh…
you're offering me a job?

615
00:32:15.727 --> 00:32:16.728
[grunts]

616
00:32:18.897 --> 00:32:20.232
You don't even like me.

617
00:32:23.235 --> 00:32:24.820
That's true.

618
00:32:28.073 --> 00:32:30.075
Okay then. I'll think about it.

619
00:32:35.831 --> 00:32:37.082
[Gordy] How's that back. Coop?

620
00:32:37.165 --> 00:32:39.251
It's getting there. Thank you.

621
00:32:39.334 --> 00:32:41.962
Um. I'm gonna send you to my man Kimo.

622
00:32:42.045 --> 00:32:44.047
He did physio for me
after my knee surgery.

623
00:32:44.131 --> 00:32:45.674
Dude's got magic hands.

624
00:32:46.425 --> 00:32:48.093
Well. I just got a Toradol shot.

625
00:32:48.177 --> 00:32:50.929
so that's doing the majority of
the heavy lifting right now.

626
00:32:51.013 --> 00:32:52.055
Yeah. that works too.

627
00:32:52.139 --> 00:32:53.182
- [laughs]
- It does.

628
00:32:53.265 --> 00:32:55.893
I had a bulging disc a few years ago.
You guys remember that?

629
00:32:57.060 --> 00:33:00.731
Oh. man. I went down the whole
rabbit hole on that one.

630
00:33:00.814 --> 00:33:03.901
Acupuncture. stem cell therapy.
you name it.

631
00:33:04.818 --> 00:33:06.737
I thought my golfing days were over.

632
00:33:07.571 --> 00:33:09.990
[Coop] When we weren't talking about
finance or sports

633
00:33:10.073 --> 00:33:11.658
or the finance of sports.

634
00:33:11.742 --> 00:33:15.204
we devoted a lot of time
to our growing laundry list of injuries.

635
00:33:15.287 --> 00:33:16.538
We were men of a certain age.

636
00:33:16.622 --> 00:33:18.665
all former jocks in some capacity.

637
00:33:18.749 --> 00:33:20.918
confronting the inevitable decline
of our bodies

638
00:33:21.001 --> 00:33:23.212
through a combination
of concierge doctors.

639
00:33:23.295 --> 00:33:27.382
cutting-edge pharmaceuticals.
trainers. and talking it all to death.

640
00:33:27.466 --> 00:33:30.302
It was just another club
we all belonged to.

641
00:33:30.385 --> 00:33:34.181
Hey. you guys get invited to this
party up at the. uh. estate on Bisbee?

642
00:33:34.264 --> 00:33:35.557
[Nick. Gordy] Yeah.

643
00:33:35.641 --> 00:33:37.184
Who is this guy?

644
00:33:37.267 --> 00:33:40.062
Owen Ashe. Hmm.

645
00:33:40.145 --> 00:33:42.606
Apparently he made a fortune on shipping.

646
00:33:42.689 --> 00:33:44.274
Paid asking on the house.

647
00:33:44.358 --> 00:33:46.276
Twenty million. All cash.

648
00:33:46.360 --> 00:33:48.487
- Jesus.
- Wow.

649
00:33:48.570 --> 00:33:49.863
Here comes the neighborhood.

650
00:33:50.697 --> 00:33:53.075
Geez. and he's throwing himself
a welcome-to-town party?

651
00:33:53.158 --> 00:33:54.618
Sounds like a douche.

652
00:33:54.701 --> 00:33:56.161
Uh. I don't know.

653
00:33:56.245 --> 00:33:59.373
It's like The Great Gatsby or something.
Could be fun.

654
00:33:59.456 --> 00:34:00.832
You know how that book ended. right?

655
00:34:00.916 --> 00:34:04.086
[all laughing]

656
00:34:04.169 --> 00:34:07.840
Well. I heard it was. uh.
Sam Levitt who made the sale.

657
00:34:07.923 --> 00:34:09.216
- Oh. yeah?
- [Brad] Mm-hmm.

658
00:34:09.299 --> 00:34:10.592
Good for her.

659
00:34:10.676 --> 00:34:11.717
[Gordy] "Good for her"?

660
00:34:12.219 --> 00:34:16.056
She desecrated the corpse of her husband
and framed you for murder.

661
00:34:16.639 --> 00:34:18.475
Did she? Who can remember?

662
00:34:18.559 --> 00:34:21.143
[Brad] Well. she knew what she was doing
when she sold Bisbee.

663
00:34:21.228 --> 00:34:23.981
She probably split. what.

664
00:34:24.523 --> 00:34:27.775
a million dollars
in commission with the listing agent?

665
00:34:27.860 --> 00:34:31.822
God. She tries to send Coop away
for life. now she's making six figures.

666
00:34:31.905 --> 00:34:33.447
That's America for you.

667
00:34:33.532 --> 00:34:34.616
[Brad] Yeah.

668
00:34:34.699 --> 00:34:36.909
I thought your guy was
trying to make it great again.

669
00:34:36.994 --> 00:34:39.079
[all laughing. clamoring]

670
00:34:40.080 --> 00:34:42.416
Hey. hey. hey. No politics at the club.

671
00:34:42.498 --> 00:34:43.583
Oh. is that a rule now?

672
00:34:49.840 --> 00:34:55.179
We are witnessing a spectacular
performance by the league's heir apparent.

673
00:34:55.262 --> 00:34:57.973
Dare I say. LeBron and Steph who?

674
00:34:58.056 --> 00:34:59.349
Calm down. Greeny.

675
00:34:59.433 --> 00:35:01.602
- Please calm down.
- Yeah. Mike. Come on. man.

676
00:35:01.685 --> 00:35:05.314
Let's see him do what the King's been
doing consistently for 20-plus years.

677
00:35:05.397 --> 00:35:07.316
I don't know.
I've never seen him do a Sombor Shuffle.

678
00:35:07.399 --> 00:35:10.319
Okay. Then how about. uh.
play defense like my guy. Kawhi?

679
00:35:10.402 --> 00:35:11.403
Oh. that's your guy now?

680
00:35:11.486 --> 00:35:13.530
Yeah. that's my guy. [laughs]
We're both Spurs legends

681
00:35:13.614 --> 00:35:15.782
- with the hardware to prove it.
- Since when do you watch sports?

682
00:35:15.866 --> 00:35:18.702
Oh. it's Nick's second week on air.
I'm just checking in.

683
00:35:19.411 --> 00:35:20.829
Uh. let's do the math on this thing.

684
00:35:20.913 --> 00:35:22.664
- Don't forget to carry the one.
- No. you're not.

685
00:35:22.748 --> 00:35:24.750
- [Nick] It was one season of overlap.
- No. I'm not.

686
00:35:24.833 --> 00:35:27.085
Hmm. Mm-mmm.

687
00:35:29.129 --> 00:35:30.297
[chuckles]

688
00:35:34.927 --> 00:35:38.222
[both moaning]

689
00:35:51.652 --> 00:35:53.987
[Barney panting]

690
00:35:59.076 --> 00:36:01.245
Um. something wrong?

691
00:36:01.870 --> 00:36:04.665
Nothing. Just keep
doing what you're doing.

692
00:36:04.748 --> 00:36:06.166
- [kisses]
- [Grace] Um…

693
00:36:08.001 --> 00:36:09.920
There's nothing
really going on down there.

694
00:36:10.003 --> 00:36:12.464
[Barney breathes heavily. sighs]

695
00:36:14.258 --> 00:36:15.425
Okay.

696
00:36:15.509 --> 00:36:16.510
Oh. Jesus.

697
00:36:18.345 --> 00:36:21.098
Okay. uh. it's okay. Barney.

698
00:36:24.309 --> 00:36:26.270
Hey. Hey. what is it?

699
00:36:26.353 --> 00:36:27.437
Talk to me.

700
00:36:27.521 --> 00:36:29.565
Do you need me to do something different?

701
00:36:29.648 --> 00:36:31.316
Yes. actually. [pants]

702
00:36:31.984 --> 00:36:34.319
I want you to not set up meetings
with your father. okay?

703
00:36:34.403 --> 00:36:36.363
I keep seeing his smug.
condescending face.

704
00:36:36.446 --> 00:36:37.990
It makes it hard to
have sex with his daughter.

705
00:36:38.073 --> 00:36:40.158
[stammers] I was just trying to help.

706
00:36:40.242 --> 00:36:43.579
And since you asked.
less pressure on the balls. okay?

707
00:36:44.121 --> 00:36:45.622
A little goes a long way down there.

708
00:36:48.292 --> 00:36:49.543
Goddamn it.

709
00:36:50.460 --> 00:36:52.171
[Ali] ♪ I remember ♪

710
00:36:53.338 --> 00:36:57.134
♪ I remember
Don't worry. worry ♪

711
00:36:58.218 --> 00:37:01.430
♪ How could I ever forget? ♪

712
00:37:01.513 --> 00:37:03.932
♪ It was the first time ♪

713
00:37:04.016 --> 00:37:05.642
[tapping foot rhythmically]

714
00:37:05.726 --> 00:37:09.563
♪ The last time that we ever met ♪

715
00:37:12.858 --> 00:37:19.656
♪ Oh. and I know the reason why
You keep this silence up ♪

716
00:37:19.740 --> 00:37:21.366
♪ Up. up ♪

717
00:37:21.450 --> 00:37:24.953
♪ No. you don't fool me ♪

718
00:37:25.037 --> 00:37:30.292
♪ 'Cause the hurt doesn't show
But the pain still grows ♪

719
00:37:30.375 --> 00:37:33.003
♪ It's no stranger to you and me ♪

720
00:37:34.129 --> 00:37:38.133
I'd been feeling a little bit off.
but then when he tried to. you know…

721
00:37:40.052 --> 00:37:41.136
- Yeah?
- Get in there.

722
00:37:41.678 --> 00:37:42.679
That's when I knew.

723
00:37:42.763 --> 00:37:44.765
Oh. Menopause.

724
00:37:44.848 --> 00:37:45.849
Yeah.

725
00:37:45.933 --> 00:37:47.559
[clicks tongue. inhales sharply] Oh.

726
00:37:49.436 --> 00:37:50.437
Hmm.

727
00:37:53.440 --> 00:37:54.691
Keep this in your purse.

728
00:37:56.652 --> 00:37:59.279
Especially if you're gonna keep
hooking up with random guys in cars.

729
00:37:59.363 --> 00:38:02.324
I don't understand.
You just carry lube around with you?

730
00:38:02.407 --> 00:38:04.576
Um. yeah. you gotta be prepared.

731
00:38:05.160 --> 00:38:06.537
And on my meds. it's just…

732
00:38:07.037 --> 00:38:10.207
It makes it. like.
drier than Death Valley down there.

733
00:38:10.290 --> 00:38:13.252
Um. as much as I'd like to hear
more about that.

734
00:38:13.335 --> 00:38:17.381
I texted you because one of
the music teachers from Mayfield

735
00:38:17.464 --> 00:38:20.425
got a touring gig and is leaving
for the rest of the semester.

736
00:38:20.509 --> 00:38:22.678
and they need a replacement.

737
00:38:23.262 --> 00:38:24.471
Oh. I'm not a teacher.

738
00:38:25.264 --> 00:38:27.683
Yeah. you're a professional musician.
which is even better.

739
00:38:27.766 --> 00:38:29.601
No. I don't know.

